{0 Migrating}

How to update code written for an older Parseff release. Each section covers
one release step; apply them in order when you skip a release.

{1 From 0.5 to 0.6}

{table
  {tr
    {th 0.5}
    {th 0.6}}
  {tr
    {td [parse_source]}
    {td {!val:Parseff.Source.parse}}}
  {tr
    {td [many p ()], [or_ a b ()] and the other combinators that take parsers}
    {td [many p], [or_ a b]; where a parser is expected, [fun () -> many p]}}
  {tr
    {td [let item = optional p]}
    {td [let item () = optional p]}}
  {tr
    {td [fail msg], final for [or_], [many] and [optional]}
    {td [cut (); fail msg] keeps it final; [fail msg] alone lets them try the
        next option}}
  {tr
    {td [warn d x] with a value [x] of your type}
    {td Declare [type Parseff.custom_error += My_warning of ...] and call
        [warn (My_warning x)]; it records into the collector given to the
        runner with [~diagnostics]}}
  {tr
    {td [warn_at d ~pos x]}
    {td [warn ~from:pos (My_warning x)]: the range goes from [pos] to the
        current position; add [~upto:pos] for an empty range at [pos]}}
  {tr
    {td ['d Diagnostics.t], ['d Diagnostics.entry]}
    {td [Diagnostics.t], [Diagnostics.entry]}}
  {tr
    {td [entry.value] as your value}
    {td [entry.value] is an [error]: match
        [{ severity = `Warning; kind = Custom (My_warning x); _ }]}}
  {tr
    {td [fold_left element op ~otherwise:default ()]}
    {td [fold_left ~otherwise:default element op]; [default] applies only when
        the first [element] fails. To fall back on any failure, use
        [or_ (fun () -> fold_left element op) (fun () -> default)]. The same
        holds for [fold_right]}}
  {tr
    {td [span], [span_to_string s]}
    {td [Slice.t], [Slice.to_string s]}}
  {tr
    {td [s.len]}
    {td [Slice.length s]}}
  {tr
    {td [s.buf.[s.off + i]]}
    {td [Slice.get s i]}}
  {tr
    {td [take_while_span], [Utf8.take_while_span]}
    {td [Slice.take_while], [Utf8.Slice.take_while]}}
  {tr
    {td [digit ()] as an [int]}
    {td [Char.code (digit ()) - Char.code '0']}}
  {tr
    {td [satisfy is_vowel ~label:"vowel"]}
    {td [satisfy ~label:"vowel" is_vowel]; the same for [Utf8.satisfy]}}
  {tr
    {td [Error { kind = Failure msg; _ }]}
    {td [Error { kind = Failed msg; _ }]}}
  {tr
    {td An exhaustive match on [error_kind]}
    {td Add a case for [Limit_exceeded { limit }]}}
  {tr
    {td [({ offset = 0; line = 1; col = 1 } : location)]}
    {td No replacement: take the [location] of an error}}
  {tr
    {td [{ line; col; offset }] on a [location]}
    {td [{ line; byte_col; _ }]}}
  {tr
    {td [e1 = e2] on two errors}
    {td [e1.range = e2.range && error_message e1 = error_message e2]}}
  {tr
    {td [skip_while_then_char p c]}
    {td [skip_while p; let _ = char c in ...]}}
  {tr
    {td [Utf8.skip_while_then_char p u]}
    {td [Utf8.skip_while p; let _ = Utf8.char u in ...]}}
  {tr
    {td [fused_sep_take ws c q]}
    {td [skip_while ws; let _ = char c in skip_while ws;
        take_while ~at_least:1 ~label:"value" q]}}
  {tr
    {td [sep_by_take ws c q]}
    {td [sep_by_take ~ws ~sep:c q]}}
  {tr
    {td [sep_by_take_span ws c q]}
    {td [Slice.sep_by_take ~ws ~sep:c q]}}
  {tr
    {td [match_regex (Re.compile r)]}
    {td [take_while p] for a character class, [recognize] with combinators for
        a longer pattern, or [Re] on the text of [take_while p] or
        [recognize p]}}
  {tr
    {td [consume s]}
    {td [literal s]}}
  {tr
    {td [let x = consume s in]}
    {td [literal s; let x = s in]}}}

Code that compiles unchanged can still behave differently:

- Invalid arguments raise [Invalid_argument] instead of being clamped: a
  negative [~at_least] or [~at_most], an [~at_most] below [~at_least], a
  [~max_depth] below 1, a negative [~from], [~upto] or note range, a negative
  [count], and a position outside the input in [location_of_position].
- Going past [~at_most] fails with [Limit_exceeded], and the failure is fatal
  like [cut (); fail]: an enclosing [or_], [optional] or repetition does not
  try another option.
- A second {!val:Parseff.Source.parse} on the same source starts after the
  input the first one consumed, instead of losing the bytes it read ahead.
  {!val:Parseff.Source.offset} gives that position.
- {!val:Parseff.Diagnostics.to_list} returns entries sorted by position.
- [skip_nested] closes a pair whose opening and closing bytes are the same,
  such as [('"', '"')], when that byte matches the innermost open bracket.

{1 From 0.4 to 0.5}

The common case is one pattern. Replace the match on the built-in variants by
a match on the error value:

{@ocaml skip[
(* 0.4 *)
match Parseff.parse input parser with
| Ok v -> v
| Error { pos; error = `Expected msg } ->
    Printf.eprintf "%d: %s\n" pos msg;
    exit 1
| Error { pos; error = `Unexpected_end_of_input } ->
    Printf.eprintf "%d: end of input\n" pos;
    exit 1
| Error _ -> exit 1

(* 0.5 *)
match Parseff.parse input parser with
| Ok v -> v
| Error e ->
    prerr_endline (Parseff.render_error ~name:"input" e);
    exit 1
]}

{table
  {tr
    {th 0.4}
    {th 0.5}}
  {tr
    {td [parse_until_end s p]}
    {td [parse s p]}}
  {tr
    {td [parse_source_until_end src p]}
    {td [parse_source src p]}}
  {tr
    {td [parse s p]}
    {td [parse ~consume:`Partial s p]}}
  {tr
    {td [parse_source src p]}
    {td [parse_source ~consume:`Partial src p]}}
  {tr
    {td [Ok (v, ds)] and [Error { error; diagnostics }]}
    {td [Ok v] and [Error e]; read the diagnostics from the collector}}
  {tr
    {td [warn x]}
    {td [let d = Diagnostics.create ()], [parse ~diagnostics:d],
        [warn d x]}}
  {tr
    {td [warn_at ~pos x]}
    {td [warn_at d ~pos x]}}
  {tr
    {td ['d Parseff.diagnostic = { pos; diagnostic }]}
    {td ['d Parseff.Diagnostics.entry = { pos; value }]}}
  {tr
    {td [error (`Too_large n)]}
    {td [type Parseff.custom_error += Too_large of int], then
        [error (Too_large n)]}}
  {tr
    {td [Error { pos; error = `Too_large n }]}
    {td [Error { kind = Custom (Too_large n); _ }], with a final [_] case}}
  {tr
    {td [('a, 'e) Parseff.result]}
    {td [('a, Parseff.error) result]}}}

- [Error { pos; error }] becomes [Error e]. The position is [e.range.start]
  and the message is [Parseff.error_message e].
- [`Failure msg] becomes [Failure msg]. Invalid UTF-8, a failed [match_regex]
  without a label and a mismatched [BE.int16] are [Expected] items
  ([valid UTF-8], [text matching the pattern], [int16be 0xFFFE]), so they
  merge with the alternatives expected at the same position.
- [`Expected msg] becomes [Expected { items; found; more }].
  [Parseff.error_message e] gives [expected A, B, or C, found X] instead of
  [expected A or expected B], sorted and without duplicates.
- [`Unexpected_end_of_input] becomes [Expected { found = End; _ }] and keeps
  what was expected.
- [`Depth_limit_exceeded msg] becomes [Depth_limit_exceeded { limit }], and
  [`Backtrack_window_exceeded info] becomes
  [Backtrack_window_exceeded { limit; oldest }].
- [Parseff.Ok] and [Parseff.Error] are [Stdlib.Ok] and [Stdlib.Error]. Code
  that writes [Ok v] and [Error e] keeps compiling.
- Tests that compare error strings change: messages name what was expected at
  the furthest failure, and [consume] fails at the first differing byte.
